Dutch is an [[official language]] of the Netherlands proper (not enshrined in the [[Constitution of the Netherlands|constitution]] but in administrative law<ref>{{Cite web |last=Koninkrijksrelaties |first=Ministerie van Binnenlandse Zaken en |title=Algemene wet bestuursrecht |url=https://wetten.overheid.nl/BWBR0005537/2024-01-01/#Hoofdstuk2_Afdeling2.2 |access-date=2024-04-24 |website=wetten.overheid.nl |language=nl |trans-quote=Art 2.6: Administrative authorities and persons working under their responsibility shall use the Dutch language, unless provided otherwise by statutory regulation. |archive-date=April 24, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240424101708/https://wetten.overheid.nl/BWBR0005537/2024-01-01/#Hoofdstuk2_Afdeling2.2 |url-status=live }}</ref>{{efn|name=notconstitution|The Dutch language does have the status of official language in the Netherlands, together with the Dutch Sign Language and West-Frisian (in Friesland). However, it is not legally enshrined in the [[Constitution of the Netherlands|Dutch constitution]], which is uncommon in the European Union.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ministerie van Binnenlandse Zaken en Koninkrijksrelaties |author-link=Ministerie van Binnenlandse Zaken en Koninkrijksrelaties |date=2016-01-11 |title=Welke erkende talen heeft Nederland? - Rijksoverheid.nl |url=https://www.rijksoverheid.nl/onderwerpen/erkende-talen/vraag-en-antwoord/onderwerpen/erkende-talen/vraag-en-antwoord/erkende-talen-nederland |access-date=2023-09-12 |website=www.rijksoverheid.nl |language=nl-NL }}{{Dead link|date=September 2023 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=DBNL |title=Grondwet 'De officiële taal van Nederland is het Nederlands' De taalartikelen in de grondwetten van de EU-landen Els Ruijsendaal Dick Wortel, Neerlandia/Nederlands van Nu. Jaargang 111 |url=https://www.dbnl.org/tekst/_nee003200701_01/_nee003200701_01_0061.php |access-date=2023-09-12 |website=DBNL |language=nl |archive-date=October 30, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231030163036/https://www.dbnl.org/tekst/_nee003200701_01/_nee003200701_01_0061.php |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="denederlandsegrondwet.nl-2018">{{Cite web |url=https://www.denederlandsegrondwet.nl/id/viivckl8ibxx/nederlandse_taal_in_de_grondwet |title=Nederlandse taal in de Grondwet |trans-title=Dutch language in the Constitution |author= |work=denederlandsegrondwet.nl |publisher=Montesquieu Instituut |date=2018 |accessdate=21 March 2023 |language=nl |archive-date=June 7, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230607182713/https://www.denederlandsegrondwet.nl/id/viivckl8ibxx/nederlandse_taal_in_de_grondwet |url-status=live }}</ref> There is almost no legally defined status of Dutch stipulated anywhere in legislation.<ref name="denederlandsegrondwet.nl-2018"/> A long series of parliamentary and public discussions in the 2010s on the question whether to enshrine Dutch as the official language of the Netherlands came to nothing, and the proposal was withdrawn again by the government in February 2018.<ref name="denederlandsegrondwet.nl-2018"/>}}), Belgium, Suriname, the Dutch Caribbean municipalities (St. Eustatius, Saba and Bonaire), [[Aruba]], [[Curaçao]] and [[Sint Maarten]]. Dutch is also an official language of several international organisations, such as the [[European Union]],<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://europa.eu/european-union/about-eu/eu-languages_en |title=EU languages |website=European Union |date=January 31, 2019 |access-date=October 20, 2019 |archive-date=October 15, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191015093122/https://europa.eu/european-union/about-eu/eu-languages_en |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Union of South American Nations]]<ref>{{Citation |title=Constitutive Treaty of the Union of South American Nations, Article 23 |url=https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Constitutive_Treaty_of_the_Union_of_South_American_Nations |quote=The official languages of the Union of South American Nations will be English, Spanish, Portuguese and Dutch. |access-date=October 20, 2019 |archive-date=July 10, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200710002607/https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Constitutive_Treaty_of_the_Union_of_South_American_Nations |url-status=live }}</ref> and the [[Caribbean Community]]. At an academic level, Dutch is taught in about 175 universities in 40 countries. About 15,000 students worldwide study Dutch at university.<ref name="vraag">{{Cite web |url=http://taalunieversum.org/onderwijs/vragen/antwoord/37/ |title=Hoeveel studenten studeren er jaarlijks Nederlands aan universiteiten buiten het taalgebied? |website=taalunieversum.org |language=nl |trans-title=How many students annually study Dutch at universities outside the language area? |access-date=November 6, 2008 |archive-date=October 29, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121029030929/http://taalunieversum.org/onderwijs/vragen/antwoord/37/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Europe ===
In Europe, Dutch is the majority language in the Netherlands (96%) and Belgium (59%) as well as a minority language in Germany and northern France's [[French Flanders]]. Though Belgium as a whole is multilingual, three of the four [[Communities, regions and language areas of Belgium|language areas]] into which the country is divided ([[Flanders]], francophone [[Wallonia]], and the [[German-speaking Community of Belgium|German-speaking Community]]) are largely monolingual, with [[Brussels]] being bilingual. The Netherlands and Belgium produce the vast majority of [[music]], [[film]]s, [[book]]s and other media written or spoken in Dutch.<ref name="trots">{{Cite web |url=http://taalunieversum.org/taalpeil/2005/hoe_trots_zijn_wij_op_het_nederlands.html |title=Hoe trots zijn wij op het Nederlands? |website=taalunieversum.org |language=nl |trans-title=How proud are we of Dutch? |access-date=August 12, 2015 |archive-date=March 19, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120319200514/http://taalunieversum.org/taalpeil/2005/hoe_trots_zijn_wij_op_het_nederlands.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Dutch is a [[Pluricentric language|monocentric language]], at least what concerns its written form, with all speakers using the same [[standardization|standard form]] (authorised by the [[Dutch Language Union]]) based on a [[Dutch orthography]] defined in the so-called "[[Green Booklet]]" authoritative dictionary and employing the [[Latin alphabet]] when writing; however, pronunciation varies between dialects. Indeed, in stark contrast to its written uniformity, Dutch lacks a unique [[Prestige (sociolinguistics)|prestige dialect]] and has a large dialectal continuum consisting of 28 main dialects, which can themselves be further divided into at least 600 distinguishable varieties.<ref>{{Cite book |title=Geschiedenis van het Nederlands |last=van der Wal |first=M. J. |date=1992 |publisher=Het Spectrum |isbn=90-274-1839-X |location=Utrecht}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |title=Morfologische Atlas van de Nederlandse Dialecten Deel II |publisher=Amsterdam University Press |year=2008 |isbn=978-9053567746 |location=Amsterdam}}</ref> In the Netherlands, the [[Hollandic dialect]] dominates in national [[broadcast media]] while in Flanders [[Brabantian dialect]] dominates in that capacity, making them in turn unofficial [[prestige dialect]]s in their respective countries.

Outside the Netherlands and Belgium, the dialect spoken in and around the German town of [[Kleve]] ([[Kleverlandish]]) is historically and genetically a [[Low Franconian]] variety. In North-Western France, the area around [[Calais]] was historically Dutch-speaking (West Flemish), of which an estimated 20,000 are daily speakers. The cities of [[Dunkirk]], [[Gravelines]] and [[Bourbourg]] only became predominantly French-speaking by the end of the 19th century. In the countryside, until [[World War I]], many elementary schools continued to teach in Dutch, and the Catholic Church continued to preach and teach the [[catechism]] in Dutch in many parishes.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Ryckeboer |first=Hugo |date=2002 |title=Dutch/Flemish in the North of France |url=http://www.multilingual-matters.net/jmmd/023/0022/jmmd0230022.pdf |journal=Journal of Multilingual and Multicultural Development |volume=23 |issue=1–2 |pages=22–35 |doi=10.1080/01434630208666452 |s2cid=144987612 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070616055025/http://www.multilingual-matters.net/jmmd/023/0022/jmmd0230022.pdf |archive-date=June 16, 2007}}</ref>

During the second half of the 19th century, Dutch was banned from all levels of education by both [[Prussia]] and France and lost most of its functions as a cultural language. In both Germany and France, the Dutch standard language is largely absent, and speakers of these Dutch dialects will use German or French in everyday speech. Dutch is not afforded legal status in France or Germany, either by the central or regional public authorities, and knowledge of the language is declining among younger generations.<ref>{{cite web|title=Flemish in France|url=http://www.uoc.edu/euromosaic/web/document/neerlandes/an/i1/i1.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20020627114548/http://www.uoc.edu/euromosaic/web/document/neerlandes/an/i1/i1.html|archive-date=June 27, 2002|access-date=August 12, 2015|publisher=Uoc.edu}}</ref>

As a foreign language, Dutch is mainly taught in primary and secondary schools in areas adjacent to the Netherlands and [[Flanders]]. In [[French Community of Belgium|French-speaking Belgium]], over 300,000 pupils are enrolled in Dutch courses, followed by over 23,000 in the [[States of Germany|German states]] of [[Lower Saxony]] and [[North Rhine-Westphalia]], and about 7,000 in the [[Regions of France|French region]] of [[Nord-Pas-de-Calais]] (of which 4,550 are in primary school).<ref name="wereldwijd" /> At an academic level, the largest number of faculties of {{lang|nl|neerlandistiek}} can be found in Germany (30 universities), followed by France (20 universities) and the United Kingdom (5 universities).<ref name="wereldwijd">{{Cite web |url=http://taalunieversum.org/taalpeil/2005/nederlands_studeren_wereldwijd.html |title=Nederlands studeren wereldwijd |website=taalunieversum.org |language=nl |trans-title=Study Dutch worldwide |access-date=August 12, 2015 |archive-date=March 19, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120319200601/http://taalunieversum.org/taalpeil/2005/nederlands_studeren_wereldwijd.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="Neerlandistiek wereldwijd">{{cite web|title=Neerlandistiek wereldwijd|url=http://neerlandistiek.taalunieversum.org/|access-date=August 12, 2015|publisher=Neerlandistiek.taalunieversum.org|language=nl|archive-date=April 2, 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120402084001/http://neerlandistiek.taalunieversum.org/|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Asia ===
{{Further|Dutch language in Indonesia|List of Dutch loanwords in Indonesian}}
[[File:Nederlandsindie.PNG|thumb|In the [[Dutch East Indies]] (present day [[Indonesia]] and [[Malacca]], Malaysia), Dutch was used by only a limited educated elite.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Groeneboer |first=K |title=Weg tot het westen. Het Nederlands voor Indie 1600–1950. |url=http://www.nrcboeken.nl/boek/in-indie-geworteld-druk-1-meijer-h |publisher=KITLEV |year=1993 |location=Leiden |access-date=November 7, 2022 |archive-date=July 18, 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110718083900/http://www.nrcboeken.nl/boek/in-indie-geworteld-druk-1-meijer-h |url-status=dead }}</ref>]]
[[File:Kantor Pos Indonesia cabang Telanaipura - Telanaipura, Kota Jambi, JA (23 April 2021).jpg|thumb|[[Indonesia]] did not adopt the Dutch language after independence. However, the [[Indonesian language]] is heavily influenced by Dutch. Seen here is ''kantor pos'' (from the Dutch ''postkantoor''), meaning ''[[post office]]''.]]
Despite the Dutch presence in Indonesia for almost 350&nbsp;years, as the Asian bulk of the [[Dutch East Indies]], the Dutch language has no official status there<ref>{{harvp|Baker|Prys Jones|1998|p=302}}</ref> and the small minority that can speak the language fluently are either educated members of the oldest generation, or employed in the legal profession such as historians, diplomats, lawyers, jurists and linguists/polyglots,<ref>{{harvp|Ammon|Dittmar|Mattheier|Trudgill|2006|p=2017}}</ref> as certain [[law code]]s are still available only in Dutch.<ref>{{harvp|Booij|1999|p=2}}</ref> Dutch is taught in various educational centres in Indonesia, the most important of which is the Erasmus Language Centre (ETC) in [[Jakarta]]. Each year, some 1,500 to 2,000 students take Dutch courses there.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://taalunieversum.org/en/dutch_worldwide/ |title=Dutch worldwide |website=taalunieversum.org |access-date=August 12, 2015 |archive-date=November 6, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121106025815/http://taalunieversum.org/en/dutch_worldwide/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In total, several thousand Indonesians study Dutch as a foreign language.<ref>{{cite web|title=Taalunie &#124; Beleidsorganisatie voor het Nederlands|url=http://taalunieversum.org/taalunie/waar_ben_je_nou_nederlands_leren_in_indonesie/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070217170723/http://taalunieversum.org/taalunie/waar_ben_je_nou_nederlands_leren_in_indonesie/|archive-date=February 17, 2007|access-date=August 12, 2015|publisher=taalunieversum.org|language=nl}}</ref> Owing to centuries of Dutch rule in Indonesia, many old documents are written in Dutch. Many universities therefore include Dutch as a source language, mainly for law and history students.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Indonesië|trans-title=Indonesia|url=http://taalunieversum.org/taalunie/indonesie/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20040315205032/http://taalunieversum.org/taalunie/indonesie/|archive-date=March 15, 2004|access-date=August 12, 2015|website=taalunieversum.org|language=nl}}</ref> In Indonesia this involves about 35,000 students.<ref name="vraag" />

Unlike other European nations, the Dutch chose not to follow a policy of language expansion amongst the indigenous peoples of their colonies.<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=I-q6gqIOQNAC |title=Language, Identity, and Marginality in Indonesia: The Changing Nature of Ritual Speech on the Island of Sumba |last=Kuipers |first=Joel Corneal |publisher=Cambridge University Press |year=1998 |page=9 |isbn=9780521624954 |access-date=June 29, 2010 |archive-date=March 24, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230324124338/https://books.google.com/books?id=I-q6gqIOQNAC |url-status=live }}</ref> In the last quarter of the 19th century, however, a local elite gained [[Language proficiency|proficiency]] in Dutch so as to meet the needs of expanding bureaucracy and business.<ref name="kol">{{Cite book|last=Groeneboer|first=Kees|url=http://www.knaw.nl/publicaties/pdf/971136.pdf|title=Westerse koloniale taalpolitiek in Azië: het Nederlands, Portugees, Spaans, Engels en Frans in vergelijkend perspectief|publisher=Koninklijke Nederlandse Akademie van Wetenschappen|year=1998|isbn=90-6984-208-4|location=Amsterdam|language=nl|access-date=August 12, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090325104245/http://www.knaw.nl/publicaties/pdf/971136.pdf|archive-date=March 25, 2009}}</ref> Nevertheless, the Dutch government remained reluctant to teach Dutch on a large scale for fear of destabilising the colony. Dutch, the language of power, was supposed to remain in the hands of the leading elite.<ref name="kol" />

After independence, Dutch was dropped as an official language and replaced by [[Indonesian language|Indonesian]], but this does not mean that Dutch has completely disappeared in Indonesia: [[Indonesian Dutch]], a regional variety of the Dutch, was still spoken by about 500,000 half-blood in Indonesia in 1985.<ref>{{cite book|url=https://www.dbnl.org/tekst/_taa008198501_01/_taa008198501_01_0002.php|title=De Nieuwe Taalgids. Jaargang 78|date=1985|publisher= [tijdschrift] De Nieuwe Taalgids|language=nl|first=M.C.|last=van den Toorn}}</ref> Yet the Indonesian language inherited many words from Dutch: words for everyday life as well as scientific and technological terms.<ref>{{harvp|Sneddon|2003|p=162}}</ref> One scholar argues that 20% of Indonesian words can be traced back to Dutch words,{{sfn|Maier|2005}} many of which are transliterated to reflect phonetic pronunciation e.g. {{lang|nl|{{Wikt-lang|nl|kantoor}}}} "office" in Indonesian is {{lang|id|kantor}}, {{lang|nl|handdoek}} "towel" in Indonesian is {{lang|id|handuk}}, or {{lang|nl|bushalte}} "bus stop" in Indonesian is {{lang|id|halte bus}}. In addition, many Indonesian words are [[calque]]s of Dutch; for example, {{lang|id|rumah sakit}} "hospital" is calqued on the Dutch {{lang|nl|ziekenhuis}} (literally "sickhouse"), {{lang|id|kebun binatang}} "zoo" on {{lang|nl|dierentuin}} (literally "animal garden"), {{lang|id|undang-undang dasar}} "constitution" from {{lang|nl|grondwet}} (literally "ground law"). These account for some of the [[Differences between Malay and Indonesian#Vocabulary differences|differences in vocabulary]] between Indonesian and Malay. Some [[languages of Indonesia|regional language]]s in Indonesia have some Dutch loanwords as well; for example, [[Sundanese language|Sundanese]] word {{lang|su|Katel}} or "frying pan" origin in Dutch is "{{lang|nl|ketel}}". The [[Javanese language|Javanese]] word for "bike/[[bicycle]]" "{{lang|jv|pit}}" can be traced back to its origin in Dutch "{{lang|nl|fiets}}". The [[Malacca]] state of [[Malaysia]] was also colonized by the Dutch in its longest period that Malacca was under foreign control. In the 19th century, the [[Dutch East Indies|East Indies]] trade started to dwindle, and with it the importance of Malacca as a trading post. The Dutch state officially ceded Malacca to the British in 1825. It took until 1957 for Malaya to gain its independence.<ref>{{cite web |date=27 September 2022 |title=Malacca: World Heritage in Malaysia |url=https://www.europeana.eu/en/blog/malacca-world-heritage-in-malaysia |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231019144322/https://www.europeana.eu/en/blog/malacca-world-heritage-in-malaysia |archive-date=October 19, 2023 |access-date=October 18, 2023 |website=europeana}}</ref> Despite this, the Dutch language is rarely spoken in Malacca or Malaysia and only limited to foreign nationals able to speak the language.

=== Oceania ===
After the declaration of independence of Indonesia, [[Western New Guinea]], the "wild east" of the [[Dutch East Indies]], remained a Dutch colony until 1962, known as [[Netherlands New Guinea]].<ref>{{Citation|title=Indonesia: Fight over the Papuans|date=December 29, 1961|url=http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,827197-1,00.html|magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130526181402/http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,827197-1,00.html|access-date=August 12, 2015|archive-date=May 26, 2013}}</ref> Despite prolonged Dutch presence, the Dutch language is not spoken by many Papuans, the colony having been ceded to Indonesia in 1963.

Dutch-speaking immigrant communities can also be found in Australia and New Zealand. The [[Census in Australia|2011 Australian census]] showed 37,248 people speaking Dutch at home.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.censusdata.abs.gov.au/ABSNavigation//download?format=xls&collection=Census&period=2006&productlabel=Language%20Spoken%20at%20Home%20by%20Sex&producttype=Census%20Tables&method=Place%20of%20Usual%20Residence&areacode=0 |title=Census Home |publisher=Censusdata.abs.gov.au |access-date=August 12, 2015 |archive-date=March 24, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230324124405/https://www.abs.gov.au/websitedbs/censushome.nsf/home/cowsredirect |url-status=live }}</ref> At the [[2006 New Zealand census]], 26,982 people, or 0.70 percent of the total population, reported to speak Dutch to sufficient fluency that they could hold an everyday conversation.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.stats.co.nz/~/media/Statistics/Publications/Census/2006-reports/quickstats-subject/Culture-Identity/quickstats-about-culture-and-identity-tables.xls |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120330181454/http://www.stats.co.nz/~/media/Statistics/Publications/Census/2006-reports/quickstats-subject/Culture-Identity/quickstats-about-culture-and-identity-tables.xls |archive-date=March 30, 2012 |title= 2006 Census Data – QuickStats About Culture and Identity – Tables |publisher=[[Statistics New Zealand]] |access-date=August 14, 2011}}</ref>

=== Americas ===
[[File:Suriname in its region.svg|thumb|The location of [[Suriname]] in [[South America]]]]
[[File:LocationNetherlandsAntillesWithAruba.png|thumb|The [[Dutch Caribbean]] at both ends of the [[Lesser Antilles]], lining the [[Caribbean Sea]]]]

In contrast to the colonies in the [[Dutch East Indies|East Indies]], from the second half of the 19th century onwards, the Netherlands envisaged the expansion of Dutch in its colonies in the [[Caribbean|West Indies]]. Until 1863, when [[slavery]] was abolished in the West Indies, slaves were forbidden to speak Dutch, with the effect that local creoles such as [[Papiamento]] and [[Sranan Tongo]] which were based not on Dutch but rather other European languages, became common in the Dutch West Indies. However, as most of the people in the [[Surinam (Dutch colony)|Colony of Surinam]] (now [[Suriname]]) worked on Dutch plantations, this reinforced the use of Dutch as a means for direct communication.<ref name="kol" /><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://taalunieversum.org/taalunie/toelichting_bij_de_overeenkomsttekst/ |title=Associatieovereenkomst Nederlandse Taalunie en de Republiek Suriname |website=taalunieversum.org |language=nl |trans-title=Association Agreement between the Dutch Language Union and the Republic of Suriname |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080828214003/http://taalunieversum.org/taalunie/toelichting_bij_de_overeenkomsttekst/ |archive-date=August 28, 2008}}</ref>

In [[Suriname]] today, Dutch is the sole official language,<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.cia.gov/the-world-factbook/countries/suriname/ |title=Suriname |work=The World Factbook |publisher=CIA |access-date=August 19, 2012 |archive-date=April 22, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210422145144/https://www.cia.gov/the-world-factbook/countries/suriname/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> and over 60&nbsp;percent of the population speaks it as a [[First language|mother tongue]].<ref name="taalgebied">{{Cite web |url=http://taalunieversum.org/taalpeil/2005/het_nederlandse_taalgebied.html |title=Het Nederlandse taalgebied |date=2005 |website=taalunieversum.org |language=nl |trans-title=The Dutch language area |access-date=November 4, 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081023045519/http://taalunieversum.org/taalpeil/2005/het_nederlandse_taalgebied.html |archive-date=October 23, 2008}}</ref> Dutch is the obligatory medium of instruction in schools in Suriname, even for non-native speakers.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://taalunieversum.org/onderwijs/suriname/ver_weg_of_dichtbij/ |title=Suriname › Documentaire |website=taalunieversum.org |language=nl |trans-title=Suriname › Documentary |access-date=August 12, 2015 |archive-date=March 19, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120319200629/http://taalunieversum.org/onderwijs/suriname/ver_weg_of_dichtbij/ |url-status=live }}</ref> A further twenty-four percent of the population speaks Dutch as a [[second language]].<ref>Source: Zevende algemene volks- en woningtelling 2004, Algemeen Bureau voor de Statistiek</ref> Suriname gained its independence from the Netherlands in 1975 and has been an associate member of the [[Dutch Language Union]] since 2004.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://taalunieversum.org/en/about_us/ |title=About us |date=January 26, 2010 |website=taalunieversum.org |access-date=August 12, 2015 |archive-date=November 6, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121106025751/http://taalunieversum.org/en/about_us/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The [[lingua franca]] of Suriname, however, is [[Sranan Tongo]],<ref>{{Cite web |title=Sranan |url=http://www.ethnologue.com/show_language.asp?code=srn |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120111042022/http://www.ethnologue.com/show_language.asp?code=srn |archive-date=January 11, 2012 |access-date=August 19, 2012 |website=Ethnologue}}</ref> spoken natively by about a fifth of the population.<ref name="trots" />{{efn|Recognition of Surinamese-Dutch (''Surinaams-Nederlands'') as an equal natiolect was expressed in 1976 by the publication of the ''Woordenboek van het Surinaams-Nederlands – een geannoteerde lijst van Surinaams-Nederlandse woorden en uitdrukkingen'' (''Dictionary of Surinam Dutch – an annotated list of Surinam-Dutch words and expressions''), see Johannes van Donselaar ''Woordenboek van het Surinaams-Nederlands – een geannoteerde lijst van Surinaams-Nederlandse woorden en uitdrukkingen'', Utrecht : Instituut A. W. de Groot voor Algemene Taalwetenschap van de Rijksuniversiteit te Utrecht (1976), Amsterdam, E.T.Rap (1977) {{ISBN|90-6005-125-4}}, published in 1989 as the ''Woordenboek van het Surinaams-Nederlands'' (''Dictionary of Surinam Dutch''), by Van Donselaar, and later by the publication of the ''Woordenboek Surinaams Nederlands'' (''Dictionary Surinam Dutch'') in 2009 (editor Renata de Bies, in cooperation with lexicologists Willy Martin en Willy Smedts), which was previously published as the ''Woordenboek van de Surinaamse Bijdrage aan het Nederlands'' (''Dictionary of the Surinam Contribution to Dutch"'').}}

Dutch is official on all 6 [[Dutch Caribbean]] islands ([[Aruba]], [[Bonaire]], [[Curaçao]], [[Sint Maarten]], [[Saba (island)|Saba]] and [[Sint Eustatius]]), but is not commonly spoken on any of the islands. Dutch is spoken as a first language by only 7% to 8% of the population,<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.cia.gov/the-world-factbook/countries/aruba/ |title=Aruba |publisher=CIA |work=The World Factbook |access-date=August 19, 2012 |archive-date=January 9, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210109084020/https://www.cia.gov/the-world-factbook/countries/aruba |url-status=dead }}</ref> although most people on the Dutch Caribbean islands can speak Dutch to varying degrees of fluency as the education system is in Dutch at some or all levels.

Now-extinct [[Dutch-based creole languages]] were formerly spoken in the [[Virgin Islands]] and [[Guyana]] ([[Negerhollands]], [[Berbice Creole Dutch|Berbice Dutch creole]], [[Skepi Creole Dutch|Skepi Dutch creole]]).

In the United States, a now extinct dialect of Dutch, [[Jersey Dutch]], spoken by descendants of 17th-century Dutch settlers in Bergen and Passaic counties, was still spoken as late as 1921.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.bartleby.com/185/a12.html |title=Jersey Dutch |publisher=Bartleby.com |access-date=August 19, 2012 |archive-date=July 2, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200702204051/https://www.bartleby.com/185/a12.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Other [[Dutch-based creole languages]] once spoken in the Americas include [[Mohawk Dutch]] (in [[Albany, New York]]), [[Berbice Creole Dutch|Berbice]] (in Guyana), [[Skepi Creole Dutch|Skepi]] (in [[Essequibo (colony)|Essequibo, Guyana]]) and [[Negerhollands]] (in the [[United States Virgin Islands]]). [[Pennsylvania Dutch]] is not a member of the set of Dutch dialects and is less misleadingly called [[Pennsylvania German language|Pennsylvania German]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://german.about.com/library/blgermyth07.htm |title=German Myth 7 – Pennsylvania Dutch or German? |date=April 10, 2012 |website=german.about.com |access-date=August 19, 2012 |archive-date=November 9, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121109181909/http://german.about.com/library/blgermyth07.htm |url-status=dead }}</ref>

[[Martin Van Buren]], the eighth [[President of the United States]], spoke Dutch natively<ref>{{cite book |last=Widmer |first=Ted |author-link=Edward L. Widmer |year=2005 |title=Martin Van Buren: The American Presidents Series: The 8th President, 1837-1841 |publisher=Times Books |location=New York, New York |isbn=978-0-8050-6922-8 |url={{google books|plainurl=y|id=YiU_kd-iAcgC}} }}</ref> and is the only U.S. president whose first language was not English. Dutch prevailed for many generations as the dominant language in parts of [[New York (state)|New York]] along the [[Hudson River]]. Another famous American born in this region who spoke Dutch as a first language was [[Sojourner Truth]].

According to the [[2000 United States census]], 150,396 people spoke Dutch at home,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.mla.org/map_data_results%26mode%3Dlang_tops%26SRVY_YEAR%3D2000%26lang_id%3D610 |title=Dutch |publisher=Mla.org |access-date=August 12, 2015 |archive-date=October 23, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151023144336/http://www.mla.org/map_data_results%26mode%3Dlang_tops%26SRVY_YEAR%3D2000%26lang_id%3D610 |url-status=live }}</ref> while according to the [[Canada 2006 Census|2006 Canadian census]], this number reaches 160,000 Dutch speakers.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www12.statcan.ca/english/census06/data/topics/RetrieveProductTable.cfm?ALEVEL=3&APATH=3&CATNO=&DETAIL=0&DIM=&DS=99&FL=0&FREE=0&GAL=0&GC=99&GK=NA&GRP=1&IPS=&METH=0&ORDER=1&PID=89189&PTYPE=88971&RL=0&S=1&ShowAll=No&StartRow=1&SUB=705&Temporal=2006&Theme=70&VID=0&VNAMEE=&VNAMEF=&GID=837928 |title=Statistics Canada 2006 (Dutch and Flemish grouped together) |date=July 4, 2011 |publisher=Statistics Canada |access-date=August 19, 2012 |archive-date=December 25, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181225074451/https://www12.statcan.gc.ca/census-recensement/2006/dp-pd/tbt/Rp-eng.cfm?LANG=E&APATH=3&DETAIL=0&DIM=0&FL=A&FREE=0&GC=0&GID=837928%20&GK=0&GRP=1&PID=89189&PRID=0&PTYPE=88971,97154&S=0&SHOWALL=0&SUB=0&Temporal=2006&THEME=70&VID=0&VNAMEE=&VNAMEF= }}</ref> At an academic level, 20 universities offer Dutch studies in the United States.<ref name="wereldwijd" /><ref name="Neerlandistiek wereldwijd"/> In Canada, Dutch is the fourth most spoken language by farmers, after English, French and German,<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/081202/dq081202a-eng.htm |title=Agriculture-population linkage data for the 2006 Census. The Daily |date=December 2, 2008 |publisher=Statistics Canada |access-date=August 19, 2012 |archive-date=October 10, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171010205657/http://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/081202/dq081202a-eng.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> and the fifth most spoken non-official language overall (by 0.6% of Canadians).<ref>Statistics Canada, ''2006 Census Profile of Federal Electoral Districts (2003 Representation Order): Language, Mobility and Migration and Immigration and Citizenship''. Ottawa, 2007, pp. 6–10.</ref>

=== Africa ===
[[File:25-centime Belgian Congo postage stamp from 1918 inscribed in Dutch only, theme is colonial expeditions.jpg|thumb|Dutch-language variant of a Belgian Congo postage stamp showing colonial expeditions]]

{{Main|Afrikaans}}{{See also|Differences between Afrikaans and Dutch}}
[[File:Stuttafords Huisgenoot.jpg|thumb|right|Standard Dutch used in a 1916 ad in South Africa before Afrikaans replaced Dutch for use in media]]
[[File:South Africa Afrikaans speakers proportion map.svg|thumb|The distribution of Afrikaans across South Africa: proportion of the population speaking Afrikaans at home:
{{legend-col
|{{legend|#EDF8E9|0–20%}}
|{{legend|#BAE4B3|20–40%}}
|{{legend|#74C476|40–60%}}
|{{legend|#31A354|60–80%}}
|{{legend|#006D2C|80–100%}}
}}]]

The largest legacy of the Dutch language lies in South Africa, which attracted large numbers of Dutch, Flemish and other northwest European farmer (in Dutch, ''[[boer]]'') settlers, all of whom were quickly assimilated.<ref name="Deumert">{{harvp|Deumert|2004}}</ref> The long isolation from the rest of the Dutch-speaking world made the Dutch as spoken in Southern Africa evolve into what is now Afrikaans.<ref name="coetzee">{{Cite book |url=http://www.dbnl.org/arch/coet003stan01_01/pag/coet003stan01_01.pdf |title=Standaard-Afrikaans |last=Coetzee |first=Abel |publisher=Pers van die Universiteit van die Witwatersrand |year=1948 |access-date=September 17, 2014 |archive-date=July 2, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200702150541/https://www.dbnl.org/arch/coet003stan01_01/pag/coet003stan01_01.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref> In 1876, the first Afrikaans newspaper called ''[[Die Afrikaanse Patriot]]'' was published in the Cape Colony.<ref>{{Cite book |title=Die verhaalskat van Ons Klyntji (1896–1905) |last=Coetzee |first=Abel Jacobus |publisher=Voortrekkerpers |year=1940 |location=Johannesburg |language=af |oclc=63430958}}</ref>

European Dutch remained the [[literary language]]<ref name="coetzee" /> until the start of the 1920s, when under pressure of [[Afrikaner nationalism]] the local "African" Dutch was preferred over the written, European-based standard.<ref name="Deumert"/> In 1925, section 137 of the 1909 constitution of the [[Union of South Africa]] was amended by Act 8 of 1925, stating "the word ''Dutch'' in article 137&nbsp;... is hereby declared to include Afrikaans".<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.archontology.org/nations/south_africa/01_laws.php |title=South Africa: Legislation: 1910–2015 |website=Archontology.org |access-date=August 12, 2015 |archive-date=July 9, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200709103320/http://www.archontology.org/nations/south_africa/01_laws.php |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite speech |last=Mpati |first=Lex |author-link=Lex Mpati |event=Inaugural Lecture |location=University of the Free State |date=October 6, 2004 |title=Transformation in the Judiciary – A Constitutional Imperative |url=http://www.supremecourtofappeal.gov.za/speeches/mpati.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070816125902/http://www.supremecourtofappeal.gov.za/speeches/mpati.pdf |archive-date=August 16, 2007}}</ref> The constitution of 1983 only listed English and Afrikaans as official languages. It is estimated that between 90% and 95% of Afrikaans vocabulary is ultimately of Dutch origin.<ref>{{harvp|Mesthrie|1995|p=214}}</ref><ref>{{harvp|Pierre|1985|p=132}}</ref>

Both languages are still largely mutually intelligible, although this relation can in some fields (such as lexicon, spelling and grammar) be asymmetric, as it is easier for Dutch speakers to understand written Afrikaans than it is for Afrikaans speakers to understand written Dutch.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Gooskens |first1=C. |last2=van Bezooijen |first2=R. |date=2006 |title=Mutual Comprehensibility of Written Afrikaans and Dutch: Symmetrical or Asymmetrical? |journal=Literary and Linguistic Computing |volume=21 |issue=4 |pages=543–557 |doi=10.1093/llc/fql036 }}</ref> Afrikaans is grammatically far less complex than Dutch, and vocabulary items are generally altered in a clearly patterned manner, e.g. {{lang|nl|vogel}} becomes {{lang|af|voël}} ("bird") and {{lang|nl|regen}} becomes {{lang|af|reën}} ("rain").<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.kwintessential.co.uk/language/about/afrikaans.html |title=The Afrikaans Language |date=August 14, 2012 |website=Kwintessential.co.uk |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160427052706/http://www.kwintessential.co.uk/language/about/afrikaans.html |archive-date=April 27, 2016 |access-date=September 21, 2016 }}</ref> In South Africa, the number of students following Dutch at university is difficult to estimate, since the academic study of Afrikaans inevitably includes the study of Dutch.<ref name="vraag" /> Elsewhere in the world, the number of people learning Dutch is relatively small.

Afrikaans is the third largest language of South Africa in terms of native speakers (~13.5%),<ref name="Key results of the 2011 census">[http://www.statssa.gov.za/Census2011/Products/Census_2011_Key_results.pdf Key results of the 2011 census]{{dead link|date=December 2016|bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, Statistics South Africa</ref> of whom 53% are [[Coloureds]] and 42.4% [[White South Africans|Whites]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.statssa.gov.za/census01/html/RSAPrimary.pdf |title=Primary tables: Census '96 and 2001 compared |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061130140908/http://www.statssa.gov.za/census01/html/RSAPrimary.pdf |archive-date=November 30, 2006 |publisher=Statistics South Africa}}</ref> In 1996, 40&nbsp;percent of South Africans reported to know Afrikaans at least at a very basic level of communication.<ref>{{harvp|Webb|2003}}</ref> It is the [[lingua franca]] in Namibia,<ref name="Deumert"/><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.cia.gov/the-world-factbook/countries/namibia/ |title=Namibia |work=The World Factbook |publisher=CIA |access-date=August 19, 2012 |archive-date=January 10, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210110010829/https://www.cia.gov/the-world-factbook/countries/namibia |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{harvp|Adegbija|1994|p=26}}</ref> where it is spoken natively in 11&nbsp;percent of households.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2007|title=About Namibia|url=http://www.grnnet.gov.na/aboutnam.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081206042235/http://www.grnnet.gov.na/aboutnam.html|archive-date=December 6, 2008|website=Government of the Republic of Namibia}}</ref> In total, Afrikaans is the [[first language]] in South Africa alone of about 7.1&nbsp;million people<ref name="Key results of the 2011 census" /> and is estimated to be a [[second language]] for at least 10&nbsp;million people worldwide,<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.ethnologue.com/show_language.asp?code=afr |title=Afrikaans |website=Ethnologue |access-date=August 19, 2012 |archive-date=March 4, 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100304114859/http://www.ethnologue.com/show_language.asp?code=afr |url-status=live }}</ref> compared to over 23&nbsp;million<ref name="taalgebied" /> and 5&nbsp;million respectively, for Dutch.<ref name="Eurobarometer Languages" />

The Dutch colonial presence elsewhere in Africa, notably the [[Dutch Gold Coast]], was too ephemeral not to be wiped out by prevailing colonising European successors. Likewise, the Belgian colonial presence in the [[Belgian Congo|Congo]] and [[Ruanda-Urundi]] ([[Rwanda]] and [[Burundi]] held under a [[League of Nations]] mandate and later a UN trust territory) left little Dutch (Flemish) legacy, as French was the main colonial language.<ref>{{in lang|fr}}[http://archives.lesoir.be/-pasopo-disent-les-ketjes-de-lubumbashi_t-19990928-Z0H9ZJ.html «Pasopo» disent les «ketjes» de Lubumbashi]; {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161018010230/http://archives.lesoir.be/-pasopo-disent-les-ketjes-de-lubumbashi_t-19990928-Z0H9ZJ.html |date=October 18, 2016 }} – [[Le Soir]], September 28, 1999</ref>

==Phonology==
{{Main|Dutch phonology}}

[[File:Intel Dutch Spoken.ogg|thumb|Spoken Dutch, with a Netherlands (Brabantian) accent]]
[[File:Brecht Decoene voice nl.ogg|thumb|Spoken Standard Dutch, with a [[West Flemish]] accent]]

For further details on different realisations of phonemes, dialectal differences and example words, see the full article at [[Dutch phonology]].

===Consonants===
Unlike other Germanic languages, Dutch has no phonological [[Aspirated consonants|aspiration of consonants]].<ref>Frans Hinskens, Johan Taeldeman, ''[https://books.google.com/books?id=t8jmBQAAQBAJ&dq=frisian%20substrate%20dutch&pg=PA66 Language and space: Dutch]; {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230324124348/https://books.google.com/books?id=t8jmBQAAQBAJ&dq=frisian%20substrate%20dutch&pg=PA66 |date=March 24, 2023 }}'', Walter de Gruyter 2014. 3110261332, 9783110261332, p.66</ref> Like most other Germanic languages, the Dutch [[consonant]] system did not undergo the [[High German consonant shift]] and has a [[syllable]] structure that allows fairly-complex [[consonant cluster]]s. Dutch also retains full use of the [[velar consonant|velar]] [[fricative]]s of [[Proto-Germanic language|Proto-Germanic]] that were lost or modified in many other Germanic languages. Dutch has [[final-obstruent devoicing]]. At the end of a word, voicing distinction is neutralised and all obstruents are pronounced voiceless. For example, Dutch ''{{lang|nl|goede}}'' (̇'good') is {{IPA|[ˈɣudə]}} but the related form ''{{lang|nl|goed}}'' is {{IPA|[ɣut]}}. Dutch shares this [[final-obstruent devoicing]] with German (the Dutch noun {{lang|nl|goud}} is pronounced {{IPA|[ɣɑut]}}, the adjective {{lang|nl|gouden}} is pronounced {{IPA|[ɣɑudə(n)]}}, like the German noun {{lang|de|Gold}}, pronounced {{IPA|[ɡɔlt]}}, adjective {{lang|de|golden}}, pronounced {{IPA|[ɡɔldn]}} vs English ''gold'' and ''golden'', both pronounced with {{IPA|[d]}}.)

Voicing of pre-vocalic initial [[voiceless alveolar fricative]]s occurs in standard Dutch like in German (Dutch {{lang|nl|zeven}}, German {{lang|de|sieben}} with {{IPA|[z]}} versus English ''seven'', Low Saxon {{lang|nds|seven}} with {{IPA|[s]}}), and also the shift {{IPA|/θ/}} → {{IPA|/d/}}. Dutch shares only with Low German the development of {{IPA|/xs/}} → {{IPA|/ss/}} (Dutch {{lang|nl|vossen}}, {{lang|nl|ossen}} and Low German {{lang|nds|Vösse}}, {{lang|nds|Ossen}} versus German {{lang|de|Füchse}}, {{lang|de|Ochsen}} and English ''foxes'', ''oxen''), and also the development of {{IPA|/ft/}} → {{IPA|/xt/}} though it is far more common in Dutch (Dutch {{lang|nl|zacht}} and Low German {{lang|nds|sacht}} versus German {{lang|de|sanft}} and English ''soft'', but Dutch {{lang|nl|kracht}} versus Low German/German {{lang|de|Kraft}} and English ''craft'').

