* {{IPA|/ɡ/}} is not a native phoneme of Dutch and occurs only in borrowed words, like {{lang|fr|baguette}}.

===Vowels===
{{more citations needed section|date=June 2026}}
Like English, Dutch did not develop [[i-mutation]] as a morphological marker and shares with most other Germanic languages the lengthening of short [[vowels]] in [[stress (linguistics)|stressed]] open [[syllables]], which has led to contrastive [[vowel length]] being used as a [[morphology (linguistics)|morphological]] marker. Dutch has an extensive [[vowel]] inventory. Vowels can be grouped as back rounded, front unrounded and front rounded. They are also traditionally distinguished by length or [[tenseness]].

Vowel length is not always considered a distinctive feature in Dutch phonology because it normally occurs with changes in vowel [[formant|quality]]. One feature or the other may be considered redundant, and some phonemic analyses prefer to treat it as an opposition of [[tenseness]]. However, even if it is not considered part of the phonemic opposition, the long/tense vowels are still realised as ''phonetically'' longer than their short counterparts. The changes in vowel quality are also not always the same in all dialects, some of which may be little difference at all, with length remaining the primary distinguishing feature. Although all older words pair vowel length with a change in vowel quality, new loanwords have reintroduced phonemic oppositions of length. Compare {{lang|nl|zonne(n)}} {{IPA|[ˈzɔnə]}} ("suns") versus {{lang|nl|zone}} {{IPA|[ˈzɔːnə]}} ("zone") versus {{lang|nl|zonen}} {{IPA|[ˈzoːnə(n)]}} ("sons"), or {{lang|nl|kroes}} {{IPA|[krus]}} ("mug") versus {{lang|nl|cruise}} {{IPA|[kruːs]}} ("cruise").

