===Diphthongs===
{{See also|IJ (digraph)}}
{{more citations needed section|date=June 2026}}
Unique to the development of Dutch is the collapse of older ''ol''/''ul''/''al'' + [[dental consonant|dental]] into ''ol'' + dental, followed by vocalisation of pre-[[consonant]]al /l/ and after a short vowel. This sound shift created the diphthong {{IPA|/ɑu/}}: Dutch {{lang|nl|goud}}, {{lang|nl|zout}} and {{lang|nl|bout}} corresponds with Low German {{lang|nds|Gold}}, {{lang|nds|Solt}}, {{lang|nds|Bolt}}; German {{lang|de|Gold}}, {{lang|de|Salz}}, {{lang|de|Balt}} and English ''gold'', ''salt'', ''bolt''. It is the most common diphthong, along with {{IPA|/ɛi œy/}}. All three are the only diphthongs commonly considered unique phonemes in Dutch. The tendency for native English speakers is to pronounce Dutch names with {{IPA|/ɛi/}} (written as ''ij'' or ''ei'') as {{IPA |/aɪ/}}, (like the English "long i") does not normally lead to confusion for native listeners, since in a number of dialects (such as in Amsterdam<ref>{{cite web|title=Onderzoek naar de volkstaal in de Jordaan|url=http://www.tussentaalenbeeld.nl/A60bb.htm|website=www.tussentaalenbeeld.nl|language=nl|access-date=February 23, 2016|archive-date=December 8, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151208111442/http://www.tussentaalenbeeld.nl/A60bb.htm|url-status=live}}</ref>), the same pronunciation is heard.

In contrast, {{IPA|/ɑi/}} and {{IPA|/ɔi/}} are rare in Dutch. The "long/tense" diphthongs are indeed realised as proper diphthongs but are generally analysed phonemically as a long/tense vowel, followed by a glide {{IPA|/j/}} or {{IPA|/ʋ/}}. All diphthongs end in a close vowel ({{IPA|/i y u/}}) and are grouped here by their first element.

