There is an ongoing process of "weakening" of strong verbs. The verb "{{lang|nl|ervaren}}" (to experience) used to be strictly a class 6 strong verb, having the past tense "{{lang|nl|ervoer}}" and participle "{{lang|nl|ervaren}}", but the weak form "{{lang|nl|ervaarde}}" for both past tense and participle is currently also in use. Some other verbs that were originally strong such as "{{lang|nl|raden}}" (to guess) and "{{lang|nl|stoten}}" (to bump), have past tense forms "{{lang|nl|ried}}" and "{{lang|nl|stiet}}" that are at present far less common than their weakened forms; "{{lang|nl|raadde}}" and "{{lang|nl|stootte}}".<ref>{{Cite web |title=Sterke werkwoorden |url=https://onzetaal.nl/taalloket/sterke-werkwoorden |access-date=2022-12-11 |website=Onze Taal |language=nl |archive-date=December 11, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221211125112/https://onzetaal.nl/taalloket/sterke-werkwoorden |url-status=live }}</ref> In most examples of such weakened verbs that were originally strong, both their strong and weak formations are deemed correct.

===Genders and cases===
As in English, the case system of Dutch and the [[subjunctive]] have largely fallen out of use, and the system has generalised the [[dative]] over the [[accusative]] [[case (linguistics)|case]] for certain [[pronoun]]s (NL: {{lang|nl|me}}, {{lang|nl|je}}; EN: ''me'', ''you''; LI: {{lang|li|mi}}, {{lang|li|di}} vs. DE: {{lang|de|mich/mir}}, {{lang|de|dich/dir}}). While standard Dutch has three [[Gender in Dutch grammar|grammatical genders]], this has few consequences and the masculine and feminine gender are usually merged into a common gender in the Netherlands but not in Belgium (EN: none; NL/LI: common and neuter; in Belgium masculine, feminine and neuter is in use).

Modern Dutch has mostly lost its case system.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.zeergoeddutch.com/cases/en |title=Grammatical Cases in Dutch |publisher=Zeer Goed Dutch |access-date=August 12, 2015 |archive-date=July 7, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150707002135/http://www.zeergoeddutch.com/cases/en |url-status=live }}</ref> However, certain idioms and expressions continue to include now archaic case declensions. The definite article has just two forms, {{lang|nl|de}} and {{lang|nl|het}}, more complex than English, which has only ''the''. The use of the older inflected form {{lang|nl|den}} in the dative and accusative, as well as use of {{lang|nl|der}} in the dative, is restricted to numerous set phrases, surnames and toponyms. But some dialects still use both, particularly "{{lang|nl|der}}" is often used instead of "{{lang|nl|haar}}" (her).

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! !! Masculine singular !! Feminine singular !! Neuter singular !! Plural (any gender)
|-
! Nominative
| {{lang|nl|de}} || {{lang|nl|de}} || {{lang|nl|het}} || {{lang|nl|de}}
|-
! Genitive
| {{lang|nl|van de}} || {{lang|nl|van de}} || {{lang|nl|van het}} || {{lang|nl|van de}}
|-
! Genitive
| {{lang|nl|des}} || {{lang|nl|der}} || {{lang|nl|des}} || {{lang|nl|der}}
|}

In modern Dutch, the genitive articles {{lang|nl|des}} and {{lang|nl|der}} in the bottom line are commonly used in [[idioms]]. Other usage is typically considered archaic, poetic or stylistic. One must know whether a noun is masculine or feminine to use them correctly. In most circumstances, the preposition {{lang|nl|van}}, the middle line, is instead used, followed by the normal article {{lang|nl|de}} or {{lang|nl|het}}, and in that case it makes no difference whether a word is masculine or feminine. For the idiomatic use of the articles in the genitive, see for example:
* Masculine singular: "{{lang|nl|'''des''' duivel'''s'''}}" (''lit'': "of the devil") (common proverbial meaning: Seething with rage)
* Feminine singular: "{{lang|nl|het woordenboek '''der''' Friese taal}}" ("the dictionary of the Frisian language")
* Neuter singular: "{{lang|nl|de vrouw '''des''' huiz'''es'''}}" ("the lady of the house")
* Plural: de voortgang "{{lang|nl|'''der''' werken}}" ("the progress of (public) works")

In contemporary usage, the [[genitive case]] still occurs a little more often with plurals than with singulars, as the plural article is {{lang|nl|der}} for all genders and no special noun inflection must be taken account of. {{lang|nl|Der}} is commonly used in order to avoid reduplication of {{lang|nl|van}}, e.g. {{lang|nl|het merendeel '''der''' gedichten '''van de''' auteur}} instead of {{lang|nl|het merendeel '''van de''' gedichten '''van de''' auteur}} ("the bulk of the author's poems").

There is also a genitive form for the pronoun {{lang|nl|die/dat}} ("that [one], those [ones]"), namely {{lang|nl|diens}} for masculine and neuter singulars (occurrences of {{lang|nl|dier}} for feminine singular and all plurals are extremely rare). Although usually avoided in common speech, this form can be used instead of possessive pronouns to avoid confusion. Compare:

* {{lang|nl|Hij vertelde over zijn zoon en '''zijn''' vrouw.}} – He spoke about his son and '''his (own)''' wife.
* {{lang|nl|Hij vertelde over zijn zoon en '''diens''' vrouw.}} – He spoke about his son and '''the latter's''' wife.

Analogically, the relative and interrogative pronoun {{lang|nl|wie}} ("who") has the genitive forms {{lang|nl|wiens}} and {{lang|nl|wier}} (corresponding to English ''whose'', but less frequent in use).

Dutch also has a range of fixed expressions that make use of the genitive articles, which can be abbreviated using apostrophes. Common examples include "{{lang|nl|'s ochtends}}" (with {{lang|nl|'s}} as abbreviation of {{lang|nl|des}}; "in the morning") and {{lang|nl|desnoods}} (lit: "of the need", translated: "if necessary").

The Dutch written grammar has simplified over the past 100 years: [[grammatical case|cases]] are now mainly used for the pronouns, such as {{lang|nl|ik}} (I), {{lang|nl|mij, me}} (me), {{lang|nl|mijn}} (my), {{lang|nl|wie}} (who), {{lang|nl|wiens}} (whose: masculine or neuter singular), {{lang|nl|wier}} (whose: feminine singular; masculine, feminine or neuter plural). Nouns and adjectives are not case inflected (except for the genitive of proper nouns (names): -s, -'s or -'). In the spoken language cases and case inflections had already gradually disappeared from a much earlier date on (probably the 15th century) as in many continental West Germanic dialects.

Inflection of adjectives is more complicated. The adjective receives no ending with indefinite neuter nouns in singular (as with {{lang|nl|een}} {{IPA|/ən/}} 'a/an'), and {{lang|nl|-e}} in all other cases. (This was also the case in Middle English, as in "a good'''e''' man".) {{lang|nl|Fiets}} belongs to the masculine/feminine category, while {{lang|nl|water}} and {{lang|nl|huis}} are neuter.

