|}

An adjective has no '''e''' if it is in the [[Predicative (adjectival or nominal)|predicative]]: {{lang|nl|De soep is koud.}}

More complex inflection is still found in certain lexicalised expressions like {{lang|nl|de heer de'''s''' hui'''zes'''}} (literally, "the man of the house"), etc. These are usually remnants of cases (in this instance, the genitive case which is still used in German, cf. {{lang|de|Der Herr des Hauses}}) and other inflections no longer in general use today. In such lexicalised expressions remnants of strong and weak nouns can be found too, e.g. {{lang|nl|in het jaar de'''s''' Her'''en'''}} (Anno Domini), where {{lang|nl|-en}} is actually the genitive ending of the weak noun. Similarly in some place names: {{lang|nl|[[Braine-le-Comte|'''&zwnj;'s'''-Grav'''en'''brakel]], [['s-Hertogenbosch|'''&zwnj;'s'''-Hertog'''en'''bosch]]}}, etc. (with weak genitives of {{lang|nl|graaf}} "count", {{lang|nl|hertog}} "duke"). Also in this case, German [[German grammar#Irregular declensions|retains this feature]].

===Word order===
Dutch shares much of its word order with German. Dutch exhibits [[subject–object–verb]] word order, but in main clauses the [[Dutch conjugation|conjugated verb]] is moved into the second position in what is known as verb second or [[V2 word order]]. This makes Dutch word order almost identical to that of German, but often different from English, which has [[subject–verb–object]] word order and has since lost the V2 word order that existed in [[Old English]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.ling.upenn.edu/~kroch/omev2-html/omev2-html.html |title=Verb Movement in Old and Middle English: Dialect Variation and Language Contact |publisher=Ling.upenn.edu |date=January 20, 1995 |access-date=August 19, 2012 |archive-date=November 23, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201123122621/http://www.ling.upenn.edu/~kroch/omev2-html/omev2-html.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

An example sentence used in some Dutch language courses and textbooks is "{{lang|nl|Ik kan mijn pen niet vinden omdat het veel te donker is}}", which translates into English word for word as "''I can my pen not find because it far too dark is''", but in standard English word order would be written "''I cannot find my pen because it is far too dark''". If the sentence is split into a main and subclause and the verbs highlighted, the logic behind the word order can be seen.

Main clause: "{{lang|nl|Ik '''kan''' mijn pen niet '''vinden'''}}"

Verb infinitives are placed in final position, but the finite, conjugated verb, in this case "{{lang|nl|kan}}" (can), is made the second element of the clause.

In subordinate clauses: "{{lang|nl|omdat het veel te donker '''is'''}}", the verb or verbs always go in the final position.

In an [[interrogative]] main clause the usual word order is: conjugated verb followed by subject; other verbs in final position:
*"{{lang|nl|Kun jij je pen niet vinden?}}" (literally "''Can you your pen not find?''") "''Can't you find your pen?''"
In the Dutch equivalent of a [[wh-question]] the word order is: interrogative pronoun (or expression) + conjugated verb + subject; other verbs in final position:
*"{{lang|nl|Waarom kun jij je pen niet vinden?}}" ("''Why can you your pen not find?''") "''Why can't you find your pen?''"
In a [[tag question]] the word order is the same as in a declarative clause:
*"{{lang|nl|Jij kunt je pen niet vinden?}}" ("''You can your pen not find?''") "''You can't find your pen?''"
A subordinate clause does not change its word order:
*"{{lang|nl|Kun jij je pen niet vinden omdat het veel te donker is?}}" ("''Can you your pen not find because it far too dark is?''") "''Can you not find your pen because it's far too dark?''"

===Diminutives===
{{See also|List of diminutives by language#Dutch}}
In Dutch, the diminutive is used extensively. The nuances of meaning expressed by the diminutive are a distinctive aspect of Dutch, and can be difficult for non-native speakers to master. It is very productive<ref name="Donaldson">{{cite book|first=Bruce|last=Donaldson|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=aFN9AgAAQBAJ&pg=PA61|title=Dutch: A Comprehensive Grammar|publisher=Routledge|year=2008|isbn=9781134082360|access-date=October 2, 2020|archive-date=March 24, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230324124339/https://books.google.com/books?id=aFN9AgAAQBAJ&pg=PA61|url-status=live}}</ref>{{rp|61}} and formed by adding one of the [[affix|suffixes]] to the noun in question, depending on the latter's phonological ending:
* {{lang|nl|-je}} for ending in -b, -c, -d, -t, -f, -g, -ch, -k, -p, -v, -x, -z or -s: {{lang|nl|neef|italic=no}} → {{lang|nl|neef|italic=no}}{{lang|nl|je}} (''male cousin'', ''nephew'')
* {{lang|nl|-pje}} for ending in -m: {{lang|nl|boom|italic=no}} (''tree'') → {{lang|nl|boom|italic=no}}{{lang|nl|pje}}
* {{lang|nl|-kje}} for ending in -ing if the preceding syllable carries the stress: {{lang|nl|koning|italic=no}} ({{lang|nl|king}}) → {{lang|nl|konin|italic=no}}{{lang|nl|kje}} (the 'ng'-sound transforms into 'nk'); but {{lang|nl|ring|italic=no}} → {{lang|nl|ring|italic=no}}{{lang|nl|etje}} (''ring''), and {{lang|nl|vondeling|italic=no}} → {{lang|nl|vondeling|italic=no}}{{lang|nl|etje}} (''foundling'') without this stress pattern
* {{Not a typo|{{lang|nl|-tje}}}} for ending in -h, -j, -l, -n, -r, -w, or a vowel other than -y: {{lang|nl|zoen|italic=no}} → {{lang|nl|zoen|italic=no}}{{lang|nl|{{Not a typo|tje}}}} (''kiss''). A single open vowel is doubled when adding "{{lang|nl|-{{Not a typo|tje}}|italic=no}}" would change the pronunciation: {{lang|nl|aut|italic=no}}{{lang|nl|o}} → {{lang|nl|aut|italic=no}}{{lang|nl|oo}}{{lang|nl|{{Not a typo|tje}}|italic=no}} (''car'').
*{{lang|nl|-′tje}} for ending in -y and for abbreviations: {{lang|nl|baby|italic=no}} → {{lang|nl|baby'{{Not a typo|tje}}|italic=no}}, {{lang|nl|cd|italic=no}} → {{lang|nl|cd'{{Not a typo|tje}}|italic=no}}, {{lang|nl|[[A4 paper|A4]]|italic=no}} → {{lang|nl|A4'{{Not a typo|tje}}|italic=no}}
* {{lang|nl|-etje}} for ending in -b, -l, -n, -ng or -r preceded by a "short" (lax) vowel: {{lang|nl|bal|italic=no}} → {{lang|nl|ball|italic=no}}{{lang|nl|etje}} (''ball''). Final consonant is doubled (except for -ng) to preserve the vowel's shortness.

The diminutive suffixes {{lang|nl|-ke}} (from which {{lang|nl|-{{Not a typo|tje}}}} has derived by [[palatalization (sound change)|palatalisation]]), {{lang|nl|-eke}}, {{lang|nl|-ske}}, {{lang|nl|-{{Not a typo|ie}}}} (only for words ending -ch, -k, -p, or -s), {{lang|nl|-kie}} (instead of {{lang|nl|-kje|italic=no}}), and {{lang|nl|-pie}} (instead of {{lang|nl|-pje|italic=no}}) are used in southern dialects, and the forms ending on {{lang|nl|-{{not a typo|ie}}|italic=no}} as well in northern urban dialects. Some of these form part of expressions that became standard language, like {{lang|nl|een makkie}}, from {{lang|nl|ge|italic=no}}{{lang|nl|mak}} = ''ease''). The noun {{lang|nl|joch|italic=no}} (''young boy'') has, exceptionally, only the diminutive form {{lang|nl|jochie|italic=no}}, also in standard Dutch. The form -ke is also found in many women's given names: Janneke, Marieke, Marijke, Mieke, Meike etc.

In Dutch, the diminutive is not restricted to nouns, but can be applied to [[numeral (linguistics)|numerals]] ({{lang|nl|met z'n tweetjes}}, "the two of us"), [[pronoun]]s ({{lang|nl|onderonsje}}, "tête-à-tête"), [[verb|verbal particle]]s ({{lang|nl|moetje}}, "shotgun marriage"), and even [[preposition]]s ({{lang|nl|toetje}}, "dessert").<ref name="Donaldson"/>{{rp|64–65}} [[Adjective]]s and [[adverbs]] commonly take diminutive forms; the former take a diminutive ending and thus function as nouns, while the latter remain adverbs and always have the diminutive with the {{lang|nl|-s}} appended, e.g. adjective: {{lang|nl|groen|italic=no}} ("green") → noun: {{lang|nl|groen|italic=no}}{{lang|nl|{{Not a typo|tje}}}} ("rookie"); adverb: {{lang|nl|even|italic=no}} ("a while") → adverb: {{lang|nl|even|italic=no}}{{lang|nl|tjes}} ("a little while").

Some nouns have two different diminutives, each with a different meaning: {{lang|nl|bloem|italic=no}} (''flower'') → {{lang|nl|bloem|italic=no}}{{lang|nl|pje}} ({{lit|small flower}}), but {{lang|nl|bloem|italic=no}}{{lang|nl|etje}} (lit. also "small flower", meaning ''[[flower bouquet|bouquet]]''). A few nouns exist solely in a diminutive form, e.g. {{lang|nl|zeepaardje|italic=no}} (''[[seahorse]]''), while many, e.g. {{lang|nl|meisje|italic=no}} (''girl''), originally a diminutive of {{lang|nl|meid|italic=no}} (''maid''), have acquired a meaning [[:nl:wikt:Categorie:Zelfstandig verkleinwoord|independent of their non-diminutive forms]]. A diminutive can sometimes be added to an uncountable noun to refer to a single portion: {{lang|nl|ijs|italic=no}} (''ice'', ''ice cream'') → {{lang|nl|ijsje|italic=no}} (''ice cream treat'', ''cone of ice cream''), {{lang|nl|bier|italic=no}} (''beer'') → {{lang|nl|biertje|italic=no}}. Some diminutive forms only exist in the plural, e.g. {{lang|nl|kleertjes|italic=no}} (''clothing'').

When used to refer to time, the Dutch diminutive form can indicate whether the person in question found it pleasant or not: {{lang|nl|een uur}}tje {{lang|nl| kletsen}} (''chatting for a "little" hour.'') The diminutive can, however, also be used pejoratively: {{lang|nl|Hij was weer eens het "mannetje"}}. (''He acted as if he was the "little" man.'')

All diminutives (even lexicalised ones like "{{lang|nl|meisje|italic=no}}" ''(girl)'') have neuter gender and take neuter concords: "{{lang|nl|dit}} {{lang|nl|kleine meisje|italic=no}}", not "{{lang|nl|deze}} {{lang|nl|kleine meisje|italic=no}}".

===Pronouns and determiners===
There are two series of personal pronouns, subject and objects pronouns. The forms on the right-hand sides within each column are the unemphatic forms; those not normally written are given in brackets. Only {{lang|nl|ons}} and {{lang|nl|u}} do not have an unemphatic form. The distinction between emphatic and unemphatic pronouns is very important in Dutch.<ref name="Donaldson"/>{{rp|67}} [[Emphatic pronoun]]s in English use the [[reflexive pronoun]] form, but are used to emphasise the subject, not to indicate a direct or indirect object. For example, "I gave (to) myself the money" is reflexive but "I myself gave the money (to someone else) " is emphatic. 
