Like English, Dutch has generalised the dative over the accusative case for all pronouns, e.g. NL '{{lang|nl|me}}', '{{lang|nl|je}}', EN 'me', 'you', vs. DE '{{lang|de|mich}}'/'{{lang|de|mir}}' '{{lang|de|dich}}'/'{{lang|de|dir}}'. There is one exception: the standard language prescribes that in the third person plural, {{lang|nl|hen}} is to be used for the direct object, and {{lang|nl|hun}} for the indirect object. This distinction was artificially introduced in the 17th century by grammarians, and is largely ignored in spoken language and not well understood by Dutch speakers. Consequently, the third person plural forms {{lang|nl|hun}} and {{lang|nl|hen}} are interchangeable in normal usage, with {{lang|nl|hun}} being more common. The shared unstressed form {{lang|nl|ze}} is also often used as both direct and indirect objects and is a useful avoidance strategy when people are unsure which form to use.<ref>{{Cite web|title=''Hun'' of ''hen''?|url=http://www.onzetaal.nl/advies/hunhen.php|access-date=May 23, 2007|website=Genootschap Onze Taal – Taaladviesdienst|language=nl|archive-date=June 6, 2007|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070606193859/http://www.onzetaal.nl/advies/hunhen.php|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Dutch also shares with English the presence of h- pronouns, e.g. NL {{lang|nl|hij}}, {{lang|nl|hem}}, {{lang|nl|haar}}, {{lang|nl|hen}}, {{lang|nl|hun}} and EN ''he'', ''him'', ''her'' vs. DE {{lang|de|er}}, {{lang|de|ihn}}, {{lang|de|ihr}}, {{lang|de|ihnen}}.

===Compounds===
[[File:Bord Hemelwaterinfiltratiegebied.JPG|thumb|The 27-letter compound ''hemelwaterinfiltratiegebied'' ([[Permeable paving|rainwater infiltration area]]) on a traffic sign in [[Zwolle]], Netherlands]]
Like most Germanic languages, Dutch forms noun [[compound (linguistics)|compounds]], where the first noun modifies the category given by the second ({{lang|nl|hondenhok}} = doghouse). Unlike English, where newer compounds or combinations of longer nouns are often written in open form with separating spaces, Dutch (like the other Germanic languages) either uses the closed form without spaces ({{lang|nl|boomhut}} = tree house) or inserts a hyphen ({{lang|nl|VVD-coryfee}} = outstanding member of the [[VVD]], a political party). Like German, Dutch allows arbitrarily long compounds, but the longer they get, the less frequent they tend to be.

The longest serious entry in the [[Van Dale]] dictionary is {{Audio|Nl-BE-Wapenstilstandsonderhandeling.oga|''wapenstilstandsonderhandeling''}} (ceasefire negotiation). Leafing through the articles of association (Statuten) one may come across a 30-letter {{Audio|Nl-BE-Vertegenwoordigingsbevoegdheid.oga|''vertegenwoordigingsbevoegdheid''}} (authorisation of representation). An even longer word cropping up in official documents is {{Audio|Nl-ziektekostenverzekeringsmaatschappij.ogg|"ziektekostenverzekeringsmaatschappij"}}(health insurance company) though the shorter {{lang|nl|zorgverzekeraar}} (health insurer) is more common.

Notwithstanding official spelling rules, some Dutch-speaking people, like some Scandinavians and German speakers, nowadays tend to write the parts of a compound separately, a practice sometimes dubbed {{lang|nl|de Engelse ziekte}} (the English disease).<ref>{{cite web|title=SOS – Signalering Onjuist Spatiegebruik|url=http://www.spatiegebruik.nl/|access-date=August 12, 2015|publisher=Spatiegebruik.nl|language=nl|archive-date=August 10, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150810073756/http://www.spatiegebruik.nl/|url-status=live}}</ref>

== Vocabulary ==
Dutch vocabulary is predominantly Germanic in origin, with loanwords accounting for 20%.<ref>{{cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=HnKeVbwTwyYC&pg=PP1 |title=Loanwords in the World's Languages: A Comparative Handbook |page=[https://books.google.com/books?id=HnKeVbwTwyYC&pg=PA349 349] |publisher=Walter de Gruyter |year=2009 |isbn=9783110218442 |access-date=January 1, 2022 |archive-date=March 24, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230324124339/https://books.google.com/books?id=HnKeVbwTwyYC&pg=PP1 |url-status=live }}</ref> The main foreign influence on Dutch vocabulary since the 12th century and culminating in the [[French period]] has been French and (northern) [[Langues d'oïl|Oïl languages]], accounting for an estimated 6.8% of all words, or more than a third of all loanwords. [[Latin]], which was spoken in the southern Low Countries for centuries and then played a major role as the language of science and religion, follows with 6.1%. High German and Low German were influential until the mid-20th century and account for 2.7%, but they are mostly unrecognisable since many have been "Dutchified": German {{lang|de|Fremdling}} → Dutch {{lang|nl|vreemdeling}}. Dutch has borrowed words from English since the mid-19th century, as a consequence of the increasing power and influence of Britain and the United States. English loanwords are about 1.5%, but continue to increase.<ref>{{cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=HnKeVbwTwyYC&pg=PP1 |title=Loanwords in the World's Languages: A Comparative Handbook |page=[https://books.google.com/books?id=HnKeVbwTwyYC&pg=PA352 352] |publisher=Walter de Gruyter |year=2009 |isbn=9783110218442 |access-date=January 1, 2022 |archive-date=March 24, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230324124339/https://books.google.com/books?id=HnKeVbwTwyYC&pg=PP1 |url-status=live }}</ref> Many English loanwords become less visible over time as they are either gradually replaced by [[calque]]s (''skyscraper'' became Dutch {{lang|nl|wolkenkrabber}}) or [[neologism]]s (''bucket list'' became {{lang|nl|loodjeslijst}}). Conversely, Dutch contributed many loanwords to English, accounting for 1.3% of its lexicon.<ref>{{cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=HnKeVbwTwyYC&pg=PP1 |title=Loanwords in the World's Languages: A Comparative Handbook |page=[https://books.google.com/books?id=HnKeVbwTwyYC&pg=PA370 370] |publisher=Walter de Gruyter |year=2009 |isbn=9783110218442 |access-date=January 1, 2022 |archive-date=March 24, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230324124339/https://books.google.com/books?id=HnKeVbwTwyYC&pg=PP1 |url-status=live }}</ref>

The main Dutch dictionary is the ''[[Van Dale|Van Dale groot woordenboek der Nederlandse taal]]'', which contains some 268,826 [[headword]]s.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.vandale.nl/ |title=Home &#124; Van Dale |publisher=Vandale.nl |access-date=August 12, 2015 |language=nl |archive-date=February 18, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120218235414/http://www.vandale.nl/opzoeken/woordenboek/?zoekwoord=stroopwafel |url-status=live }}</ref> In the field of [[linguistics]], the 45,000-page {{lang|nl|[[Woordenboek der Nederlandsche Taal]]}} is also widely used. That scholarly endeavour took 147 years to complete and contains all recorded Dutch words from the [[Early Middle Ages]] onward.

==Spelling and writing system==
{{Main|Dutch orthography|History of Dutch orthography|Dutch six-dot Braille|Dutch eight-dot Braille}}

[[File:Amsterdamse tram - De Red Crosser - from Flickr 2838709455 cropped lijnbus.jpg|thumb|Dutch uses the [[digraph (orthography)|digraph]] {{vr|[[IJ (digraph)|ij]]}} as a single letter and it can be seen in several variations. Here, a marking saying {{wikt-lang|nl|lijnbus}} ("line/route" + "bus"; the tram lane also serves as bus road).]]

Dutch is written using the [[Latin script]]. Dutch uses one additional character beyond the standard alphabet, the [[digraph (orthography)|digraph]] {{vr|[[IJ (digraph)|ij]]}}. It has a relatively high proportion of doubled letters, both vowels and consonants, due to the formation of compound words and also to the spelling devices for distinguishing the many vowel sounds in the Dutch language. An example of five consecutive doubled letters is the word {{lang|nl|voorraaddoos}} ([[food storage container]]). The [[Diaeresis (diacritic)|diaeresis]] (Dutch: {{lang|nl|trema}}) is used to mark vowels that are pronounced separately when involving a pre- or suffix, and a [[hyphen]] is used when the problem occurs in compound words, e.g. {{lang|nl|beïnvloed}} (influenced), {{lang|nl|de zeeën}} (the seas) but {{lang|nl|zee-eend}} (scoter; lit. sea duck). Generally, other [[diacritic|diacritical marks]] occur only in loanwords. However, the [[acute accent]] can also be used for emphasis or to differentiate between two forms, and its most common use is to differentiate between the indefinite [[article (grammar)|article]] {{lang|nl|een}} {{IPA|/ən/}} "a, an" and the numeral {{lang|nl|één}} {{IPA|/eːn/}} "one".

Since the 1980s, the Dutch Language Union has been given the mandate to review and make recommendations on the official spelling of Dutch. Spelling reforms undertaken by the union occurred in 1995 and 2005. In the Netherlands, the official spelling is currently given legal basis by the Spelling Act of September 15, 2005.{{efn|See {{Citation |title=Spellingwet |url=https://wetten.overheid.nl/BWBR0018784/2010-10-10 |language=nl |access-date=October 24, 2019 |archive-date=October 24, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191024063848/https://wetten.overheid.nl/BWBR0018784/2010-10-10/ |url-status=live }}}}{{efn|This came into force on February 22, 2006, replacing the Act on the Spelling of the Dutch Language of February 14, 1947. see {{Citation |title=Wet voorschriften schrijfwijze Nederlandsche taal |url=https://wetten.overheid.nl/BWBR0002027/ |language=nl |access-date=October 24, 2019 |archive-date=March 24, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230324124407/https://wetten.overheid.nl/BWBR0002027/1997-02-21 |url-status=live }}}} The Spelling Act gives the Committee of Ministers of the Dutch Language Union the authority to determine the spelling of Dutch by ministerial decision. In addition, the law requires that this spelling be followed "at the governmental bodies, at educational institutions funded from the public purse, as well as at the exams for which legal requirements have been established". In other cases, it is recommended, but it is not mandatory to follow the official spelling. The Decree on the Spelling Regulations 2005 of 2006 contains the annexed spelling rules decided by the Committee of Ministers on April 25, 2005.{{efn|see {{Citation |title=Besluit bekendmaking spellingvoorschriften 2005 |url=https://wetten.overheid.nl/BWBR0019422/ |language=nl |access-date=October 24, 2019 |archive-date=March 24, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230324124856/https://wetten.overheid.nl/BWBR0019422/2006-05-01 |url-status=live }}}}{{efn|This decree entered into force on August 1, 2006, replacing the Spelling Decree of June 19, 1996. see {{Citation |title=Spellingbesluit |url=https://wetten.overheid.nl/BWBR0008116/ |language=nl |access-date=October 24, 2019 |archive-date=March 24, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230324124856/https://wetten.overheid.nl/BWBR0008116/1996-08-01 |url-status=live }}}} In Flanders, the same spelling rules are currently applied by the Decree of the Flemish Government Establishing the Rules of the Official Spelling and Grammar of the Dutch language of June 30, 2006.{{efn|See {{Citation |title=Besluit van de Vlaamse Regering tot vaststelling van de regels van de officiële spelling en spraakkunst van de Nederlandse taal |url=http://www.ejustice.just.fgov.be/eli/besluit/2006/06/30/2009036011/staatsblad |language=nl |access-date=October 24, 2019 |archive-date=October 27, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201027075933/http://www.ejustice.just.fgov.be/eli/besluit/2006/06/30/2009036011/staatsblad |url-status=live }}}}

The {{lang|nl|[[Word list of the Dutch language|Woordenlijst Nederlandse taal]]}}, more commonly known as {{lang|nl|het groene boekje}} (i.e. "the green booklet", because of its colour), is the authoritative orthographic word list (without definitions) of the Dutch Language Union; a version with definitions can be had as {{lang|nl|Het Groene Woordenboek}}; both are published by [[Sdu (publishing company)|Sdu]].

